  "fingerprint": "A series circuit contains a 4 ohm resistor, a 12 ohm inductive reactance, and an 9 ohm capacitive reactance. What is the total magnitude of the impedance?~~25 ohms|7 ohms|5 ohms|15 ohms~~2~~Inductive reactance opposes current by generating a back electromotive force that causes voltage to lead current by ninety degrees.|Capacitive reactance opposes current by accumulating electrostatic charge, causing voltage to lag ninety degrees behind alternating current.|Because one leads by ninety degrees and the other lags by ninety, they are exactly one hundred eighty degrees apart.|On the complex plane, inductive reactance points straight up as positive j, while capacitive reactance points downward as negative j.~~Inductive reactance opposes current by generating a back electromotive force that causes voltage to lead current by ninety degrees.|Capacitive reactance opposes current by accumulating electrostatic charge, causing voltage to lag ninety degrees behind alternating current.|Because one leads by ninety degrees and the other lags by ninety, they are exactly one hundred eighty degrees apart.|On the complex plane, inductive reactance points straight up as positive j, while capacitive reactance points downward as negative j.|When connected in series, their opposing reactances cancel each other out directly through simple algebraic subtraction.|Subtracting the 9 ohm capacitive reactance from the 12 ohm inductive reactance yields a net inductive reactance of 3 ohms.|Net impedance is the vector sum of real resistance and net imaginary reactance using the Pythagorean theorem.|Squaring 4 ohms resistance plus squaring 3 ohms reactance equals 25, giving a square root of 5 ohms.|At series resonance where both reactances balance completely, net reactance collapses to zero leaving pure resistance alone."
